/*!
    \example simple_publisher
    \title Simple Publisher
    \ingroup qtros2-examples
    \meta sortkey {10}
    \brief Publishing a geometry_msgs/PoseStamped message from QML with a
    single publisher.

    \c{Simple Publisher} is the smallest useful Qt ROS2 application: it wraps one
    \l{Qt ROS2 geometry_msgs QML Types}{PoseStampedPublisher} in a \l{Qt ROS2 Core
    QML Types}{Node} and publishes a randomly generated
    \c geometry_msgs/PoseStamped every time you press a button. It is the natural
    starting point for learning how the bridge turns ROS 2 topics into QML types.

    \image simple_publisher/simple_publisher.png "The Simple Publisher window: an editable topic, a Publish Random Pose button, a subscriber counter, and the last published message."

    \section1 Running the Example

    \list 1
        \li Source your ROS 2 environment, then launch Qt Creator from the same
            shell so it can find the generated QML modules.
        \li Open \c examples/simple_publisher/CMakeLists.txt in Qt Creator, then
            build and run.
        \li Watch the published messages from a separate, ROS-sourced terminal:
            \badcode
            ros2 topic echo /simple_publisher_pose
            \endcode
    \endlist

    Pair this example with \l{Simple Subscriber} to see both ends of a topic in
    two Qt windows.

    \section1 Declaring a Node and a Publisher

    Everything ROS-facing lives inside a \c Node. A publisher is declared as a
    child of the node; the node owns the underlying \c rclcpp publisher and tears
    it down automatically:

    \qml
    import QtRos2.GeometryMsgs

    Node {
        id: rosNode
        nodeName: "simple_publisher_node"

        PoseStampedPublisher {
            id: posePublisher
            topic: topicField.text
            autoStamp: autoStampSwitch.checked
        }
    }
    \endqml

    A few things worth noting:

    \list
        \li The \c topic is bound to a text field, so it can be edited live — the
            publisher re-advertises on the new topic when the binding changes.
        \li \c Node exposes an \c initialized property. The example keeps the
            \e Publish button disabled until \c{rosNode.initialized} is true, so
            the application never tries to publish before the ROS 2 context is
            ready.
        \li \c{posePublisher.subscriberCount} reports how many subscribers are
            currently connected — a convenient way to confirm the topic is wired
            up before you publish.
    \endlist

    \section1 Publishing With a JavaScript Object Literal

    Message value types are constructible from plain JavaScript objects, so you
    never have to build a message imperatively. Nested fields map directly to
    nested objects, and field names use Qt's \c camelCase spelling
    (\c frameId, not \c frame_id):

    \qml
    posePublisher.publish({
        header: {
            frameId: frameField.text
            // header.stamp is intentionally omitted — see below
        },
        pose: {
            position:    { x: 1.0, y: 2.0, z: 0.5 },
            orientation: { x: 0.0, y: 0.0, z: 0.0, w: 1.0 }
        }
    })
    \endqml

    \section1 Automatic Timestamps

    \c PoseStampedPublisher is a \e stamped publisher, which means its message
    carries a \c std_msgs/Header. When \c autoStamp is enabled (the default), the
    publisher fills \c header.stamp from the ROS 2 \e node clock at publish time —
    not from Qt's clock — so downstream nodes and TF see a consistent ROS time
    source. That is why the snippet above leaves \c header.stamp unset. The
    example exposes a switch so you can turn \c autoStamp off and supply your own
    stamp when you need to.

    \sa {Simple Subscriber}, {Qt ROS2 geometry_msgs QML Types}
*/
